{
  "number": 8,
  "title": "Modes and Form",
  "guide": {
    "summary": "Unit 8 covers modes and other scales, and the formal structures of common-practice and popular music, from binary and ternary forms to sonata form and the twelve-bar blues.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Modes and other scales",
        "body": "The church modes can be heard on the white keys: Ionian (C, major), Dorian (D: natural minor with raised 6), Phrygian (E: natural minor with lowered 2), Lydian (F: major with raised 4), Mixolydian (G: major with lowered 7), Aeolian (A, natural minor), and Locrian (B, diminished tonic triad, rarely used). Other scales include the major pentatonic (1 2 3 5 6), minor pentatonic, the whole-tone scale (six notes, all whole steps, no perfect fifths), the chromatic scale, and the blues scale. To identify a mode, compare it with the major or natural minor scale on the same tonic."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Small forms",
        "body": "Binary form (AB, each section usually repeated) often has a first section ending away from tonic (e.g., in the dominant). Rounded binary returns part of A at the end of the second section (A B A'). Ternary form (ABA) has a contrasting, self-contained middle section and a full return. Strophic songs repeat the same music for each verse; through-composed songs set each verse to new music. Theme and variations alters a theme in melody, rhythm, harmony, or texture."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Larger and popular forms",
        "body": "Rondo alternates a recurring refrain with contrasting episodes (ABACA or ABACABA). Sonata form has an exposition (first theme in tonic, transition, second theme in the dominant or relative major, closing), a development that fragments and modulates, and a recapitulation that returns themes in the tonic, sometimes with a coda. The twelve-bar blues follows I I I I | IV IV I I | V IV I I (with variants), and popular songs use verse–chorus structures."
      }
    ],
    "keyTakeaways": [
      "Dorian = minor ↑6; Phrygian = minor ↓2.",
      "Lydian = major ↑4; Mixolydian = major ↓7.",
      "Whole-tone: all whole steps.",
      "Binary vs rounded binary vs ternary.",
      "Sonata: exposition, development, recapitulation.",
      "Twelve-bar blues pattern."
    ],
    "commonMistakes": [
      "Confusing Dorian and Aeolian.",
      "Calling rounded binary 'ternary' when the return is partial and within section 2.",
      "Placing the second theme of the recapitulation in the dominant.",
      "Mislabeling strophic vs through-composed."
    ],
    "examTips": [
      "Compare modes to major/minor on the same tonic.",
      "Listen for returns of opening material.",
      "Track key areas in sonata form."
    ],
    "cramSheet": "**Modes:** Ionian · Dorian (m ↑6) · Phrygian (m ↓2) · Lydian (M ↑4) · Mixolydian (M ↓7) · Aeolian · Locrian\n\n**Scales:** pentatonic · whole-tone · chromatic · blues\n\n**Forms:** binary · rounded binary · ternary · strophic · through-composed · variations · rondo · sonata · 12-bar blues"
  },
